  "verse": "How you look back on these old times today, which according to your present cognitions and terms were bearing in them blatant misery and boundless unright as well as unbelievable unhumaneness, is how your descendants will one day look back on the past, if they think about your time, your actions which are far from the creational laws, your unintellect and your irrationality, your contempt for humankind, the destruction of the nature, the eradication of many plants, fishes, birds, animals and other creatures and the destruction of the climate.",
  "verseSrc": "Chapter 28, Verse 379",
  "hinge": "§28:379 does not describe a punishment. It describes **how the record will read** — the descendants looking back at *\"contempt for humankind\"* and *\"the destruction of the climate\"* as one entry, because the text refuses to separate cruelty to people from cruelty to the world that carries them. A summit that cannot name the cause cannot be judged on its ambition; it can only be judged on what it left in the record, and what Antalya leaves is a walkout. **First,** make the **deletion traceable**: publish which delegation, in which session, removed the term, so that the failure has an author rather than an atmosphere — attribution is the only sanction a consensus process has ever had. **Second,** move **adaptation finance** into a body that does not require unanimity, because a fund that needs the consent of the states most exposed to the outcome is a fund that will always be empty. **Third,** stop treating the plenary as the only instrument: bilateral and plurilateral agreements, carbon-market linkage and national plans do not need a consensus room, and the energy transition has advanced further through those channels in fifteen years than through any COP text."
