   "**Linking carbon markets makes climate policy a shared asset, which means a shared hostage** — the world's largest linked emissions trading system, agreed in the same week COP31 collapsed for want of a single noun; the direct effect is that the UK's carbon price is now partly set abroad; the compound consequence is that any future divergence — a UK decision to soften its cap, an EU decision to impose a border levy — becomes a treaty dispute, so the instrument meant to lower costs raises the cost of changing your mind",
   "**A youth mobility scheme capped at 100,000 with no benefits and no dependants satisfies neither side's constituency** — three years, ages 18 to 31, reciprocal, launching in 2030; the direct effect is that the scheme is small enough not to alarm and small enough not to matter; the compound consequence is that both the free-movement wing and the control wing will claim betrayal, and the scheme will be judged on a number it was designed never to exceed",
   "**A deal this size, this fast, in this parliament, is a bet that growth arrives before the electorate notices the alignment** — a prime minister facing an opposition positioning on sovereignty and a business lobby that wants access; the direct effect is that the politics of the deal are being fought at the CBI conference rather than in the details; the compound consequence is that the agreement's survival depends on an economic result it cannot guarantee, which makes it a wager on the model rather than a settlement of the argument"
